  data:  # 2d20
    kind: invariant  # 2d20
    title: "verify-claims returns confirmed for a boundary reachable through modules the catalogue cannot adjudicate (reproduced: poetry 'never sends data over the network', 14 files import requests)"  # 2d20
    status: violated  # 2d20
    priority: 1  # 2d20
    parent:  # 2d20
    tags:  # 2d20
    - io_boundary  # 2d20
    - verify_claims  # 2d20
    - adr_0016  # 2d20
    - false_confirm  # 2d20
    - rct_blocker  # 2d20
    isbefore: []  # 2d20
    duplicate_of: []  # 2d20
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 2d20
    pr_ref:  # 2d20
    description: "REPRODUCED LIVE on two UNMODIFIED upstream repos, with a positive control firing in\nthe SAME run (so this is not the analysis being blind and saying so):\n\n  $ hypergumbo verify-claims ~/ALL_REPOS/curriculum_repos/poetry/src/poetry\n    x [CONTROL-FSREAD] violated - 20 fs_read chain(s) found      <- the run WORKED\n    v [NET] This service never sends data over the network.\n        Verdict: confirmed.  No net_send chains found.           <- FALSE\n\npoetry imports requests in 14 files and calls requests.Session()/.get()/.post().\nfull-stack-fastapi-template/backend reproduces the database half: `confirmed` for\n\"never writes to a database\" against crud.py:14-15 session.add(db_obj);\nsession.commit(), read directly from source.\n\nMECHANISM, read in code. compute_boundary_coverage (verify_claims.py) modelled two\nblind spots (WI-kajil / INV-bitig), both at the resolution of \"did this language\nproduce ANY call edges\". Python produces hundreds of thousands, so coverage read\ncomplete and the clean verdict was confirmable. This is EXACTLY the residual already\nrecorded in cli.py's own docstring (\"closing it needs a signal with resolution finer\nthan 'any', e.g. the share of method-construct call edges that resolve to something\nthe catalogue can match\") and pinned as the xfail\ntest_language_with_a_token_call_edge_still_falsely_confirms. That residual was filed\nas a Kotlin problem; it is not language-specific, and Python -- the best-supported\nlanguage and the likely RCT substrate -- reproduces it on real repos.\n\nRELATION TO THE FILED L1 CLAIM. Found while adjudicating INV-linub's 2026-08-08\n\"32.9% sqlalchemy / ZERO catalogue entries\" paragraph. That paragraph is a cohort\nartifact (see the correction filed on INV-linub); this is the real user-facing defect\nsitting underneath it.\n\nNOT MEASURED: any non-Python language; the false-positive direction (does the new\ngate downgrade a verdict that was genuinely earned?) beyond the fixtures and the two\nrepos above; whether the taint arm's behaviour changes on any repo other than\nhypergumbo itself."  # 2d20
    fields:  # 2d20
      statement: "Every clean (zero-chain) boundary verdict reported as confirmed must be backed by an analysis that could have classified the I/O -- including calls into third-party modules, whose absence from the catalogue makes them unexamined rather than examined-and-clean."  # 2d20
      root_cause: "compute_boundary_coverage (packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/verify_claims.py) modelled exactly two blind spots, both at the resolution of 'did this language produce ANY call edges' -- zero call edges overall, or a supported language with zero. Python produces hundreds of thousands on any real repo, so both pass and a clean boundary verdict stays confirmable no matter how much of the repo's I/O left through an uncatalogued third-party module. Read in code and reproduced end-to-end on two upstream repos. cli.py's own docstring already recorded this residual and named the fix shape ('a signal with resolution finer than any'), filing it as Kotlin-specific; it is not language-specific."  # 2d20
